---
import DemoLessonLayout from "../../../layouts/DemoLessonLayout.astro";
const createFile =
  'python3 -c \'from pathlib import Path; Path("hello.txt").write_bytes(b"HELLO")\'';
---

<DemoLessonLayout
  title="Rehearse a TCP transfer"
  description="Send five bytes on one laptop and practice a two-minute explanation."
  downloads
  next={{ path: "tutorials/tcp-demo/hotspot/", label: "Try two laptops" }}
>
  <section class="lab-section" aria-labelledby="loopback">
    <div class="section-heading">
      <h2 id="loopback" tabindex="-1">
        Rehearse on one laptop
      </h2>
      <p>
        Save the two scripts in a fresh folder and open two terminals in that
        folder. You need Python 3. The server defaults to loopback (
        <code>127.0.0.1</code>), so the demonstration works with Wi-Fi off.
      </p>
    </div>
    <ol class="lab-steps">
      <li>
        <strong>Server terminal. Start listening.</strong>
        <pre class="lab-code"><code>python3 server.py</code></pre>
        <p>
          Wait for <code>Waiting on 127.0.0.1:5001</code>.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Client terminal. Create and send the sample.</strong>
        <pre class="lab-code"><code>{createFile + "\npython3 client.py hello.txt"}</code></pre>
        <p>
          The client prints <code>OK. The server saved 5 bytes.</code> The
          received copy is <code>received-file.bin</code>.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Check the copy and reset.</strong>
        <pre class="lab-code"><code>shasum -a 256 hello.txt received-file.bin</code></pre>
        <p>
          On macOS, the two hashes should match. On Linux you can also use
          <code>sha256sum</code>. To repeat, rename the received copy before
          starting the server again. The server handles one transfer per run and
          protects existing files from being overwritten.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ol>
    <details>
      <summary>Explain the two checks</summary>
      <p>
        The sender's 40-byte header contains eight bytes for the file length and
        32 for the SHA-256 digest. The server reads enough bytes to parse that
        header, then reads the announced file length. TCP delivers those bytes
        as a stream, so the receiver can get the header and file across several
        reads.
      </p>
      <p>
        The hash checks what arrived. The one-byte receipt comes from the
        program after it verifies and saves the file. A TCP acknowledgment is
        generated by the network stack and can arrive before that work is
        finished.
      </p>
    </details>
    <h3>Two-minute run of show</h3>
    <ol class="lab-steps">
      <li>
        <strong>0:00–0:25.</strong> Show the waiting server and name the IP and
        port.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>0:25–0:55.</strong> Run the client. Explain that it sends a
        40-byte header with the file size and SHA-256 fingerprint, followed by
        the file. TCP delivers an ordered stream of bytes.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>0:55–1:30.</strong> Point to the saved file and the OK line. The
        server checks the received fingerprint, saves the file, and returns a
        one-byte application receipt.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>1:30–2:00.</strong> Show the matching hashes if time allows.
        Explain that the fingerprint checks the contents even when two files
        have the same size.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <div class="note">
      <h3>Prepare the fallback before you present</h3>
      <p>
        Rehearse twice on the presentation laptop with Wi-Fi off. Enlarge both
        terminals, pre-type the client command, and save a local recording or
        screenshots from a successful rehearsal. If an error appears or five
        seconds pass without OK, switch to the recording and keep explaining.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</DemoLessonLayout>
